[Tai Chi Mindfulness Ball 15-Minute Guided Practice]

Next, following the music, we begin a 15-minute mindful ball-rotation practice.

[Preparing with Reverence]

Please sit upright with a stable posture. Bring your palms together, gather your body and mind, and let us cultivate the Four Immeasurable Minds:

May all beings have happiness and the causes of happiness.

May all beings be free from suffering and the causes of suffering.

May all beings experience the supreme joy beyond sorrow, body and mind at ease.

May all beings abide in equanimity, free from attachment and aversion.

[Settling the Body, Calming the Breath]

Find your seat---either upright in a chair or sit cross-legged on the floor.

Let the spine rise naturally. Hold the Tai Chi ball with both hands, resting it lightly against your lower abdomen.

Close your eyes, settle the mind, calm the breath, and take three deep breaths---

inhale... exhale...

inhale... exhale...

inhale... exhale...

Adjust the center of the ball to align with your heart.

Keep your hands aligned on opposite sides of the ball.

Notice the different sensations where your palms and the backs of your hands meet the ball.

Clear and vivid, knowing each moment.

While rotating the ball, bring awareness to your body:

Gently lift the crown of your head; let the shoulders melt downward and the elbows drop with ease; soften the waist and sink into the hips.

Keep your thighs level with the ground and your lower legs vertical.

Feet flat on the floor, toes pointing forward.

While turning the ball, keep the center steady and your hands balanced on opposite sides.

Turn slowly and steadily, again and again.

Let both hands turn slowly together.

Right now, where is your mind?

The object of this beginning practice is the opposite point where each hand meets the ball.

Aware of this moment, let the mind follow the hands, and the hands follow the ball.

Naturally selfless, tension and release in balance, softness and strength together.

Through turning the ball, let steadiness of attention open the door of awareness.

Keep the center of the ball steady and the opposite points balanced.

Turn slowly and steadily, again and again.

Now, once again, bring awareness back to your body:

Spine upright. The crown of your head slightly lifted. Shoulders relaxed, elbows at ease. Waist and hips settled, grounded.

Be aware of the three ninety-degree angles of your body.

While turning the ball, move slowly and evenly, keeping the speed steady and the center of the ball stable.

Not too fast, not too slow; not too high, not too low; not leaning left or right.

Let the waist move the shoulders, the shoulders move the elbows, the elbows move the wrists, and the wrists move the hands.

Stay focused, aware, natural and unforced.

Selfless, without controlling, the Middle Way of balanced tension and release, softness and strength together.

[Concluding the Practice]

Now, stop rotating the ball. Place it on your lap with both hands.

Let the body soften. Let the breath return to its natural rhythm. Keep your awareness at the dantian.

[Dedication of Merit]

Please bring your palms together. With sincerity, let us dedicate the merit:

May the merits of our deeds reach every part of the world.

Sentient beings large and small, all attain enlightenment.

[Gentle Reminder]

Avoid washing your hands with cold water right after practice, to prevent catching a chill.

If practicing with others, you may gently pat each other's shoulders and upper back using relaxed, open palms.

Release tension in the muscles and seal in the warmth.

Rejoice in the merit of everyone practicing the Tai Chi Mindfulness Ball!
